To effectively compare morphological complexity across languages, it is essential to comprehend the characteristics of complex forms within specific systems and to identify a measurement method that captures these traits. This study investigates three proposed corpus-based indicators: Mean Length of Morpheme (MLM), Mean Number of Morphemes Per Word (MNMPW), and Mean Inflectional Type Per Lexeme (MITPL). Through a cross-linguistic analysis of 18 typologically diverse families, we examined how these metrics correlate with one another and with MNMPW, a proxy for morphological richness. Our findings indicate that MNMPW serves as a robust indicator for richness in both inflectional and non-inflectional paradigms, as well as for the density of morphological content within individual words. However, MNMPW does not reliably reflect the complexity of morphological categories, nor do MITPL and MNMPW accurately predict the complexity of semantic categories. In contrast, MITPL is a strong proxy for the complexity of morphosyntactic categories, while both MITPL and MLM capture the complexity of meaning associated with morphemes. Finally, we discuss the implications of these results for cross-linguistic research on complex morphology. The underlying data and corpora are accessible via https://github.com/alexei-gorin/complexity_metrics. The underlying code can also be found at https://github.com/alexei-gorin/complexity_metrics/blob/master/Correlating_and_validating_indicators_of_Morphological_complexity.ipynb. This research was funded by the Social Sciences and Humanities Research Council of Canada (SSHR), grant number 435-2019-0174. Additional support was provided by the University of Alberta's Department of Linguistics, the Faculty of Arts, and the Department of Computer Science. The funding agencies had no role in the design, execution, or publication of this research. The views expressed herein are solely those of the authors and do not necessarily reflect those of the SSHRC. 